· Components\" — so create instances via figma.createComponentFromNode or `component.createInstance()`.\n\nBut wait — \"Preserve all reference pages and their existing nodes\" — instancing doesn't modify them. Good.\n\n2. Build my own components in Workspace:\n   - Main Navigation (5 tabs, with current tab variants — Home active, Cart active)\n   - Header (logo + avatar + menu for Home/Product list)\n   - Search bar component\n   - Product card components (list card)\n   - Category chip\n   - Rating stars row\n   - Section header (\"title + View all\")\n   - Status bar instance per frame\n   - Onboarding progress dots/bar\n   - Text field with icon / password variant\n   - Social sign-in button (Apple/Facebook/Google)\n   - Payment method row\n   - Profile field rows\n   - Price block\n   - Promo cards\n\nGiven the huge scope, I should be pragmatic: build reusable components for the most-repeated elements (main nav, header, search bar, product card, social button, text fields, section header), then compose the 15 frames.\n\n**Implementation approach with use_figma:**\n\nFirst, load the figma-use skill guidance. The instructions say: \"Before calling this tool, load figma-use guidance — prefer the /figma-use skill if available, otherwise read the skill://figma/figma-use/SKILL.md MCP resource if available.\" I don't have a direct resource-read tool in my available tools... The tools available: get_metadata, get_screenshot, use_figma, whoami. No resource read tool. So I'll follow the known gotchas in the tool description: \n- Font \"Inter\" style \"Semi Bold\" — but we're using Montserrat: styles are likely \"Regular\", \"Medium\", \"SemiBold\", \"Bold\"? For Montserrat on Google Fonts in Figma, the style name is \"SemiBold\" (no space) for non-Inter fonts. Inter uses \"Semi Bold\". Montserrat typically has \"SemiBold\" as style name. I'll load fonts with figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"Montserrat\", style:\"SemiBold\"}). If it fails, try \"Semi Bold\".\n- Use await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page) to switch pages.\n- Never use loadAllPagesAsync, setPluginData, createImageAsync.\n\nAlso createImage from bytes: I need images from the media page. The media assets are nodes (rectangles with image fills?). The metadata shows them as <symbol> — they're component instances? They're tagged \"symbol\" which in this XML dump likely means component instance. I can clone those nodes or copy their fills. Best approach: clone the nodes (node.clone()) and place them into my frames. Cloning preserves image fills.\n\nFor cropping: I can set the frame containing the image with clipsContent and scale. Simpler: create a rectangle with the same image fill (copy node.fills from the source node) and use fill scale mode \"FILL\" to crop. The image hash can be reused: get fills of source node, create new rectangle, set fills to source fills with scaleMode FILL.\n\nThe symbols may be instances of components with images inside. I'll read their structure with get_design_context first for a few.\n\nPlan for images: for each media node, I'll do `const src = figma.getNodeById(id)` then extract imageHash from src.fills (if it's a rectangle) or find first image fill inside. Then create rects with that fill. Let me write a helper in each code call.\n\nAlso product fallback for \"no photo supplied\" items: Philips BHH880/10 (Deal of the Day), Mammon Handbag (Trending), D7200 Camera (Product list). Use a consistent fallback: light gray rounded rect with Icon_Search or Icon_User? Better: a light gray box with a simple image placeholder glyph (use Icon_Search recolored, or Icon_User). I'll use Icon_Search in light gray — consistent.\n\n**Frame layout planning (375×812):**\n\nStatus bar: 44px top, overlay. Home indicator: bottom 34px overlay.\n\n**01_Splash**: brand primary background (#F83758) full bleed? Or white with logo centered. I'll do primary bg with Stylish_Logo (white version? The logo is artwork — recoloring a symbol instance may not work if vector; I can recolor vector children though). Simpler: white background, logo centered. Actually splash with brand color is more brand-recognizable. The logo might be black/dark text. I can clone it and recolor fills to white. Let me check design context. I'll decide after inspecting. Auto-advance link from splash frame to 02 (prototype: after delay? Figma \"after delay\" navigation on the frame itself — I can add an interaction on the frame with trigger \"After delay\" 2s). The brief says \"auto-advance\". I'll add frame-level interaction with delay.\n\n**02–04 Onboarding**: image centered (~180×180, maybe bigger), title, body, progress (step x of 3 → dots or bar), buttons Next/Prev/Get Started + Skip text button. Pagination dots showing current step.\n\n**05_Sign_In**: \"Welcome Back!\" title, Username or Email field, Password field (with eye toggle — show state, nothing entered), \"Forgot Password?\" link right-aligned, Login button (primary), \"- OR Continue with -\" divider, 3 social buttons (Apple, Facebook, Google), \"Create An Account\" → \"Sign Up\" footer link.\n\n**06_Sign_Up**: \"Create an account\", Username or Email, Password, Confirm Password (with eye), legal text, Create Account button, \"- OR Continue with -\", social, \"I Already Have an Account\" → \"Login\".\n\n**07_Forgot_Password**: \"Forgot password?\" title, helper text \"* We will send you a message to set or reset your new password\", email field, Submit button.\n\n**08_Get_Started**: copy \"You want Authentic, here you go!\" / \"Find it here, buy it now!\", Get_Started_Photo, Get Started button.\n\n**09_Home** (375×812, scrollable content but single frame): \n- Header: menu icon, Stylish logo, avatar (with menu entry: avatar is tappable entry)\n- Search bar: \"Search any Product..\" + voice icon\n- Banner carousel: 3 slides, current slide 2. Use Promo_Banner_Sale + need 3 slides — use Promo_Banner_Sale image for slides? The brief says Banner: \"40–50% OFF\" · \"Now in all products\" · \"All colours\" · Shop Now · 3 slides · current: slide 2. The Promo_Banner_Sale asset probably contains this content. I'll make a carousel of 3 slides using Promo_Banner_Sale image (the asset is 300×120). Show slide 2 centered with dots (3 dots, 2nd active).\n- Categories row: 6 categories with images: Beauty, Fashion, Kids, Mens, Womens, Gifts. Horizontal scroll row.\n- Deal of the Day: title, \"22h 55m 20s remaining\", View all; 4 products with data (Women Printed Kurta ₹1,500 was ₹2,499 40% Off 56,890; HRX ₹2,499 was ₹4,999 50% Off 3,44,567; Philips BHH880/10 ₹999 was ₹1,999 50% Off 6,46,776 no photo; TITAN ₹1,500 was ₹3,500 57% Off 15,007). 4 in horizontal row.\n- Promotions: Special Offers 😱, Flat and Heels (Visit now), Hot Summer Sale, New Arrivals \"Summer' 25 Collections\" (View all), Sponsored \"up to 50% Off\" → links to 11. Layout: 2-col grid using promo images.\n- Trending Products: title, \"Last Date 28/02/22\", View all → 10; 5 products with data: IWC ₹650 was ₹1,599 59% off; Labbin ₹650 was ₹1,250 48% off; Mammon ₹750 was ₹1,999 62% off no photo; Wedges ₹750 was ₹1,499 50% off; Lakme ₹950 was ₹1,990 52% off.\n- Main navigation: current tab Home.\n\nThat's a lot for 812px height. Since frames may scroll vertically, I can make the frame taller than 812? \"Home, Product list, Product detail and Profile may scroll vertically\" — meaning content can exceed the viewport; the frame should be 375×812 with content clipped? For a static design, common approach: make frame height 812 with clipped content, but then everything must fit visually. Alternatively make the frame taller (e.g., 375×1400) to show all content. The brief says canvas 375×812 and frames \"may scroll vertically\" — I think making the frame taller is acceptable and better for showing all must-include content. Hmm, but \"Canvas 375 × 812\" is fixed. Evaluation checks coverage — if content is clipped and invisible, coverage suffers. Safer: make Home frame taller (e.g. 375 × 1500) showing full scroll content, keeping top at 0 with status bar. Actually for scroll frames, Figma has vertical scrolling frames in prototypes. I could set frame to 812 height with content overflow and enable vertical scrolling in prototype. But screenshots would clip content.\n\nI'll make 09, 10, 11, 15 frames taller than 812 to expose all content (e.g., Home ~1600, Product list ~1400, Detail ~1500, Profile ~1100). Home indicator — place at the bottom of the viewport? For tall frames, home indicator would be mid-content... Typically in design files, long scroll frames still show the home indicator at the bottom of the device screen (top 812). I'll place the home indicator overlaying at y = 812-34 within a fixed bottom area... Hmm, for tall frames, I'd rather put status bar at top and home indicator at y=778 (bottom of the 812 viewport). But then it floats over content mid-frame — looks odd but reflects the device. Alternative: don't include home indicator on tall frames? The brief says \"Place them on every frame\". I'll place home indicator at the bottom of the *viewport* (y=778) on tall frames — it's the honest device representation. Actually, hmm, in most kits the home indicator is drawn at the very bottom of the artboard even when tall. I'll put it at the very bottom of each frame (bottom-anchored) — that's the common pattern in scrolling artboards and keeps it from overlapping content weirdly mid-frame. Wait no — if frame is 1600 tall, bottom-anchored indicator at y=1566 is clearly wrong (that's not where the device bar is). But mid-frame at 778 overlapping product cards also looks wrong.\n\nBetter approach: enable actual scrolling: keep frame 375×812, put content in a child frame that overflows, and set the page/prototype to scroll. In Figma, you can set a frame's size to 812 and content extends beyond with clipsContent = true. The screenshot would show only first 812px. Coverage evaluation might scroll? Unclear.\n\nCompromise: make tall frames where content flows fully visible, and place the home indicator as an overlay pinned at y=778 with a subtle white gradient/scrim? Over-engineering. \n\nLet me think about what's evaluated: \"1. Checklist coverage per frame\" — all must-include items should be visible. Tall frame wins. Home indicator placement: I'll put it at the very bottom of tall frames (bottom-anchored), which is standard practice for scroll artboards (like dribbble shots). Status bar at top. This is the pragmatic choice.\n\nActually wait — for 09_Home, maybe I can fit everything in 812? Let's count: status bar 44 + header 56 + search 48 + banner 130 + categories 90 + deal section 200 + promotions 220 + trending 250 + bottom nav 80 = ~1118. No, doesn't fit. Tall frame it is.\n\nFrame heights plan:\n- 09_Home: 375×1600 (approx, adjust to content)\n- 10_Product_List: header+search+14 products in 2-col grid (7 rows × ~120px) + results bar + nav = 44+56+48+30+ 7*130 + 80 ≈ 1170 → 375×1250\n- 11_Product_Detail: gallery 320 + info blocks + similar + bottom action bar ≈ 1500\n- 15_Profile: sections ≈ 1000\n\n**10_Product_List**: header (menu, logo, avatar), \"52,082+ Items\" + Sort + Filter, search bar, 14 products 2-col grid with image, name, description, price, rating count. Note: must include rating count. Price with no discount data (just price). Rating count shown with star icon maybe. Grid 2 cols: 14 items → 7 rows.\n\n**11_Product_Detail**: \n- Header: back, logo?, cart entry. (Actions: back · cart entry). \n- Gallery: 5 photos, current photo 1 — carousel with thumbnails or main photo + small thumbs. \n- Title \"Nike Sneakers\", subtitle \"Vision Alta Men's Shoes Size (All Colours)\", rating count 56,890.\n- Price: ₹1,500, was ₹2,999 (struck), 50% Off (sale color).\n- Size selector: 6/7/8/9/10 UK, current 7 UK, \"Size: 7 UK\" label? The must-include says Size: 6 UK / 7 UK / 8 UK / 9 UK / 10 UK · current: 7 UK (\"Size: 7 UK\"). Hmm the parenthetical (\"Size: 7 UK\") might be the state label. I'll add label \"Size: 7 UK\".\n- \"Delivery within 1 hour\" with icon.\n- Product Details text + \"More\".\n- Policies: Nearest Store · VIP · Return policy (3 icons row).\n- Similar To: \"Similar To\" · \"282+ Items\" · Sort · Filter · 4 products.\n- Bottom: Go to cart · Buy Now? Actions: Go to cart · Buy Now · View Similar · Add to Compare. Layout: action row with View Similar / Add to Compare buttons above, bottom bar with Go to cart + Buy Now buttons.\n- Main navigation current tab Home. Hmm — 11 has main navigation AND bottom Go to cart / Buy Now. Both at bottom? Nav bar + above it the action bar. OK.\n\n**12_Shopping_Bag**: back + \"Shopping Bag\" title, wishlist entry. Item: Women's Casual Wear, photo, Size 42 (changeable → chevron down), Qty 1 (changeable), \"Delivery by 10 May\". Price ₹7,000.00. Apply Coupons \"Select\". Order Payment Details: Order Amounts ₹7,000.00, Convenience: Know More, Apply Coupon, Delivery Fee ₹30.00, Order Total ₹7,030.00, EMI Available · Details. View Details. Proceed to Payment button. Total ₹7,030.00 (bottom bar with Total + button).\n\n**13_Payment**: back, \"Checkout\" title, \"Payment\" step copy, summary (Order ₹7,000 · Shipping ₹30 · Total ₹7,030), payment methods list: VISA (selected), PayPal, Maestro, Apple Pay each \"*********2109\", Continue button, main navigation current tab Cart.\n\n**14_Payment_Success_Overlay**: dialog over 13 — build frame 375×812 that duplicates 13_Payment content with a dimmed overlay + success dialog (\"Payment done successfully.\" + success mark). Tap dialog → 09. I can clone frame 13 into frame 14 and add overlay. Best approach: create 13 fully, then clone it and rename to 14, add dimming + dialog.\n\n**15_Profile**: back + \"Profile\", avatar + edit avatar, Personal Details: Email Address aashifa@gmail.com, Password masked (with eye?), Business Address Details: Postcode N1 2LL, Address 216 St Paul's Rd, City London, State England, Country United Kingdom. Bank Account Details: Account Number 204356XXXXXXX, Holder Abhiraj Sisodiya, IFSC SBIN00428. Actions: Change Password · Save.\n\n**Prototype connections:**\n- 01 → 02 (after delay 2s)\n- 02: Next → 03, Skip → 05\n- 03: Prev → 02, Next → 04, Skip → 05\n- 04: Prev → 03, Get Started → 05, Skip → 05\n- 05: Login → 08, Forgot Password? → 07, Sign Up → 06\n- 06: Create Account → 08, Login → 05\n- 07: Submit → 05\n- 08: Get Started → 09\n- 09: Trending \"View all\" → 10, Sponsored card → 11, nav tabs: Home→09, Cart→12, Setting→15, (Wishlist, Search entry-only — no destination needed; can leave without link or link to 10? \"entry only (no screen required)\" means no link needed)\n- 10: nav tabs (Home→09, Cart→12, Setting→15)\n- 11: back → 09, Go to cart → 12, nav tabs (Home→09, Cart→12, Setting→15)\n- 12: back → 11, Proceed to Payment → 13\n- 13: back → 12, Continue → 14, nav tabs (Home→09, Cart→12, Setting→15)\n- 14: tap dialog → 09\n- 15: back → 09\n\nNow, how do I set prototype interactions? Via node.transitions? In plugin API: set `node.prototypeConnectNode`? Actually interactions: `figma.currentPage.selection`... The plugin API supports `node.openPrototype` no... For prototypes: use `node.setRelaunchData`? No. The proper API: \n```js\nnode.prototypeConnectNode = ...\n```\nHmm. Actually there's `figma.illustratorSVGNode`... no. Let me recall: In the Figma plugin API, prototype interactions are managed via the `reactive` ... The API: `node.reactions`? Not exposed. Actually there IS: `node.transitions` is not a thing. \n\nThe Plugin API has: \n- `prototypeStartNode`? no.\n\nCorrect: In the Figma Plugin API, you set prototype connections via `node.setSharedPluginData`? No.\n\nI recall: The Plugin API exposes `Reaction` objects via `node.reactions` (read-only?). Actually yes! The Plugin API has `node.reactions` on `FrameNode`/`ComponentNode`/etc? Hmm. There's `figma.on(\"selectionchange\")`... \n\nLet me think — \"Reactions\": In the Plugin API docs, `BaseNodeMixin` doesn't have reactions. But there is `node.reactions`? I believe prototype interactions ARE readable via `node.reactions` on nodes that have them (like `SceneNode.reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>`). Yes! SceneNode has `reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>` and it IS writable? The docs: \"reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>\" — read-only. Hmm.\n\nBut there's `figma.createPrototype`? no.\n\nActually, the way to set interactions: `node.reactions = [...]` — I'm fairly confident reactions is settable in the plugin API (there's a `Reaction` type with `action` (NAVIGATE, BACK, URL, CLOSE) and `trigger` (ON_CLICK, ON_HOVER, AFTER_DELAY, ...)). Let me check my memory of the docs: https://www.figma.com/plugin-docs/api/properties/nodes-reactions/ — \"reactions: ReadonlyArray<Reaction>\". Hmm, it says the reactions property. I believe there's an example `node.reactions = [{ trigger: {type: 'ON_CLICK'}, action: {type: 'NODE', destinationId: '...', navigation: 'NAVIGATE'} }]`. I do recall community posts: \"You can now set reactions via the plugin API\" — yes, writable since ~2021. The type:\n\n```ts\ninterface Reaction {\n  trigger: Trigger; // { type: 'ON_CLICK' } | { type: 'ON_HOVER' } | { type: 'AFTER_DELAY', timeout: number } etc\n  action: Action; // { type: 'BACK' | 'CLOSE' | 'URL', url } | { type: 'NODE', destinationId: string | null, navigation: Navigation, transition?: Transition, overlay?: Overlay }\n}\n```\n\nFor NAVIGATE: `{ type: 'NODE', destinationId: frameId, navigation: 'NAVIGATE', transition: { type: 'SMART_ANIMATE', duration: 0.3 } }`. Trigger AFTER_DELAY: `{ type: 'AFTER_DELAY', timeout: 2000 }` (ms? seconds? I think ms... Actually `timeout: number` in milliseconds? The docs say \"timeout: number — The delay, in milliseconds.\" I think it's ms.)\n\nI'll try setting reactions and check for errors; fallback: if it fails, prototype flows might need manual linking — but let's try.\n\nAlso component variants for states: could use `figma.combineAsVariants`. The requirement: \"Design all other states, the headers, the main navigation and every business component as