t rendered as a white blob? The screenshot shows the badge with a white shape that could be a settings gear filled white. It's small; acceptable either way. But if tint broke it (filled square), better replace with fresh + set white? Fresh instance default color is dark, on red badge that'd look bad. Actually current render shows white icon on red badge and looks fine. Leave it.\n\nWait — but is it rendering as white gear or white square? At 14×14 in screenshot it's ~14px. From the image, the badge shows a white round-ish shape. I'll leave it — risk of making it worse.\n\n**08 (14:222)**: title t08 w=481 h=34 ar=WIDTH_AND_HEIGHT at x=20,y=90 — clipped. Fix: set textAutoResize=\"HEIGHT\", resize(335, h). Text is top-level in frame → legal.\n\nNow the replacement approach: for each broken instance, create fresh instance of same master, same parent, same index position, same x/y, same size (24×24 etc.), then remove old. No tint.\n\nFor the nested chevrons in 12 (\"Item Card\" frame, \"Payment Details Card\" frame): parent is a regular frame — fine to append.\n\nInsert order: use parent.insertChild(index, newInstance) to preserve z-order. Get index via parent.children.indexOf(old). Simpler: appendChild then set x/y — z-order may change but these icons sit on top anyway; the header icons are above everything else at that location, so appending is fine as long as nothing opaque covers them. The header area has no overlapping opaque elements. Chevrons in Item Card: the card frame has children (text etc.), chevron at x=184,y=48 — appended on top is fine.\n\nActually to be safe, I'll use insertChild with the old index, then remove old. But careful: insertChild index semantics with removed node — do: const idx = parent.children.indexOf(old); parent.insertChild(idx, fresh); old.remove(). That preserves position in z-order.\n\nThen resize fresh instance: icons are 24×24 default; the chevrons were 12×12; settings 14×14. Fresh instance default size = component size. Resize to recorded w/h.\n\nOne concern: will fresh instances render correctly (glyph, not square)? The components themselves render fine elsewhere (07 back chevron OK, 09 icons OK). The bug came from my tint() overrides. Fresh = no override = fine.\n\nBut wait — for 11's top-right, the kit design likely wants a cart icon top-right on product detail? Original kit: product detail has back left, and ... it's fine, keep cart.\n\nAlso should I tint the fresh ones to match design (e.g., dark #1A1D1E)? Component default already correct.\n\nFor 12's chevrons: they were tinted gray. Fresh default = dark. The kit shows dark chevrons next to Size/Qty — acceptable. Keep fresh default.\n\nFor Chevron_Right in Payment Details Card: fresh default dark — fine.\n\nNow 08 title fix: t08.textAutoResize=\"HEIGHT\"; t08.resize(335, t08.height). Font must be loaded before resize? Resizing text requires font loaded — I load all Montserrat styles anyway.\n\nAlso check 08's subtitle/body below title — after wrapping to 2 lines, title height grows from 34 to ~62; elements below (photo at y=190) — title at y=90 + 62 = 152 < 190 ✓ no overlap.\n\nWrite the fix call now. Also keep the helper pattern. All in one call:\n\n```js\nconst page = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(\"5:2\");\nawait figma.setCurrentPageAsync(page);\nfor (const s of [\"Regular\",\"Medium\",\"SemiBold\",\"Bold\"]) await figma.loadFontAsync({family:\"Montserrat\",style:s});\nasync function M(id){return await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(id);}\nconst fixes = [\n  [\"19:527\",\"4:60\"],[\"19:527\",\"4:43\"],[\"19:527\",\"4:150\"],[\"19:527\",\"4:155\"],[\"19:527\",\"4:160\"],\n  [\"20:619\",\"4:60\"],[\"20:619\",\"4:36\"],[\"20:619\",\"4:116\"],[\"20:619\",\"4:116\"],[\"20:619\",\"4:111\"],\n  [\"21:637\",\"4:60\"],[\"21:707\",\"4:60\"],[\"21:731\",\"4:60\"]\n];\nlet replaced = [];\nconst byMaster = {};\nfor (const [fid, mid] of fixes) {\n  const f = await M(fid);\n  const insts = f.findAll(n => n.type===\"INSTANCE\" && n.mainComponent && n.mainComponent.id===mid);\n  for (const old of insts) {\n    const master = old.mainComponent;\n    const par = old.parent;\n    const idx = par.children.indexOf(old);\n    const x = old.x, y = old.y, w = old.width, h = old.height;\n    const fresh = master.createInstance();\n    par.insertChild(idx, fresh);\n    fresh.x = x; fresh.y = y; fresh.resize(w, h);\n    replaced.push({f:fid, m:mid, x:Math.round(x), y:Math.round(y), w:Math.round(fresh.width), h:Math.round(fresh.height)});\n    old.remove();\n  }\n}\n// 08 title wrap\nconst t08 = (await M(\"14:222\")).findOne(n => n.type===\"TEXT\" && n.characters.indexOf(\"You want\")===0);\nt08.textAutoResize = \"HEIGHT\";\nt08.resize(335, t08.height);\nreturn JSON.stringify({count: replaced.length, replaced, t08:{w:Math.round(t08.width), h:Math.round(t08.height)}});\n```\n\nWait — findAll on frame f finds nested instances too (e.g. nav icons inside Main Navigation instance have mid 4:36 etc.!). The nav icons are nested INSIDE the Main Navigation instance — does findAll return them? findAll traverses all descendants... Actually for instances, findAll does NOT traverse inside instance children (instance children are not accessible via findAll on the parent? Hmm — actually findAll DOES include children of instances in some cases... no. Let me think: The Figma plugin API: node.findAll returns descendants. For an INSTANCE node, its children are the instance's internal nodes, which ARE traversable (you can read them), and frame.findAll does traverse INTO instances? \n\nFrom the inspection output above: the findAll on 19:527 DID return \"Icon_Home\" mid 4:31 with par \"Main Navigation\" — yes! It traversed into the Main Navigation instance. So my fix loop WOULD replace nav icons too if their master matches a fix master. My fixes include 4:60, 4:43, 4:36, 4:150, 4:155, 4:160, 4:116, 4:111. Nav contains 4:31, 4:36, 4:43, 4:49, 4:55! So 4:36 and 4:43 inside nav instances would be caught — but replacing a node NESTED inside an instance with a